===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|broker}}, {{m|enm|brokour}}, {{m|enm|brocour}}, from {{etyl|xno|en}} {{m|fro|brocour||small trader}} (compare also {{m|fro|abroker||to act as a broker}}), from {{etyl|odt|en}} {{m|odt|*brokere||one who determines the usages of trade, manager}}, from {{m|odt|broke}}, {{m|odt|bruyck}}, {{m|odt|breuck||use, usage, trade}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*brūkiz||use, custom}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*bhrug-||to use, enjoy}}, equivalent to {{suffix|brook|er|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{cog|gml|brukere||a broker}}, {{cog|da|bruger||a broker, user, handler}}, {{cog|sv|bruk||use, custom, trade, business}}, {{cog|ang|broc||use, profit, advantage, foredeal}}. Compare also {{cog|fr|brocanter||to deal in second-hand goods}} from the same Germanic source. More at {{l|en|brook}}.

